window.NTI=window.NTI||{};(window.NTI.docs=window.NTI.docs||{})["aiops-03-the-observability-stack-complete-study-guide"]={"html":"<p>Here's your merged, single-file study guide. Save it as <code>observability-complete-study-guide.md</code> — everything below is one coherent document.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-markdown\"><span class=\"hljs-section\"># The Observability Stack: Complete Study Guide</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki + Promtail · Prometheus + Exporters · Grafana — deployed on Minikube with Helm**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Scope of this document:**</span> Observability fundamentals → the three signal types → Loki/Promtail</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; architecture → Prometheus/exporters → Grafana → the full Minikube + Helm deployment → querying</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; (LogQL/PromQL) → app integration → troubleshooting → cleanup → mental model → interview prep.</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; Everything here reflects one concrete lab: a Minikube cluster, a dedicated `observability`</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; namespace, and two Helm charts (`kube-prometheus-stack` and `loki-stack`).</span>\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Table of Contents</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">What is Observability?</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-1--what-is-observability</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Logs vs Metrics vs Traces</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-2--logs-vs-metrics-vs-traces</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Logs: Loki + Promtail</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-3--logs-loki--promtail</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Metrics: Prometheus + Exporters</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-4--metrics-prometheus--exporters</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Traces (context for the next step)</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-5--traces-context-for-the-next-step</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">6.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Visualization: Grafana</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-6--visualization-grafana</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">7.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">How It All Fits Together in Kubernetes</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-7--how-it-all-fits-together-in-kubernetes</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">8.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment: Minikube + Helm</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-8--deployment-minikube--helm</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">9.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Querying the Data: LogQL and PromQL</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-9--querying-the-data-logql-and-promql</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">10.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Application Integration</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-10--application-integration</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">11.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Troubleshooting Playbook</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-11--troubleshooting-playbook</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">12.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Cleanup</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-12--cleanup</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">13.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">The Mental Model (Architecture Diagram)</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-13--the-mental-model-architecture-diagram</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">14.</span> [<span class=\"hljs-string\">Interview Prep: Concepts, Commands, Quiz</span>](<span class=\"hljs-link\">#part-14--interview-prep</span>)\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 1 — What is Observability?</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Observability**</span> is the ability to understand what is happening inside a system by examining the\ndata it produces — <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**without having to ship new code to ask a new question.**</span>\n\nThat last clause is the whole point. You instrument the system <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*once*</span>; after that, any new question\n(\"why was latency high at 2 AM?\", \"which pod threw the error?\") is answered by querying data that\nalready exists, not by redeploying with extra print statements.\n\nObservability rests on <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**four core pillars**</span>:\n\n| Pillar | One-liner |\n|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Logs**</span> | Discrete, timestamped records of events |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Metrics**</span> | Numeric measurements over time |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Traces**</span> | The path a single request takes across services |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Visualization**</span> | Turns raw signals into dashboards a human can act on |\n\nEach signal exists to answer a <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*different kind of question*</span> — memorize the questions:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Logs**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*\"What happened, and what did it say?\"*</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Metrics**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*\"How much, how fast, how often?\"*</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Traces**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*\"Where did the time go?\"*</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Visualization**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*how a human consumes all of the above.*</span>\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 2 — Logs vs Metrics vs Traces</span>\n\nThe three signal types are complementary, not redundant. This table is the single most\nexam-worthy artifact in this guide:\n\n| | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Logs**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Metrics**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Traces**</span> |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Question**</span> | What happened, and what did it say? | How much, how fast, how often? | Where did the time go? |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Nature**</span> | Discrete, timestamped event records | Numeric measurements over time | Full journey of one request across services |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Shape**</span> | Free-form text lines | Time series (name + labels + values) | Request path through service hops |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Stored by (this stack)**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*Not deployed in this lab*</span> (Tempo / Jaeger / Zipkin would be) |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Query language**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**LogQL**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**PromQL**</span> | (trace UI) |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Best for**</span> | Reading the <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*exact*</span> content of an event (\"show me the stack trace\") | Trends, rates, aggregation, alerting, dashboards | Latency breakdown across a multi-service request |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**How they work together (the classic debugging flow):**</span>\na <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**metric**</span> alert fires (\"error rate jumped\") → a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**trace**</span> shows which hop got slow → a\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**log**</span> line tells you the exact exception. <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana**</span> is the pane of glass where all of this\ncomes together — and because Loki uses <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*the exact same label model as Prometheus and Kubernetes*</span>,\nyou can pivot from a metric on a pod straight to that pod's logs.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### The Core Stack at a Glance</span>\n\n| Component | Role | Installed by | Runs as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki**</span> | Stores and indexes logs | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`loki-stack`</span> | Log backend |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Promtail**</span> | Tails container logs on every node and ships them to Loki | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`loki-stack`</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**DaemonSet**</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus**</span> | Scrapes and stores metrics as time series | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> | Metrics backend |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**node-exporter**</span> | Exposes host-level metrics | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**DaemonSet**</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**kube-state-metrics**</span> | Exposes Kubernetes object state metrics | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> | Deployment |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Alertmanager**</span> | Routes PromQL alert conditions to Slack/email/PagerDuty | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> | <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*Disabled in this lab*</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana**</span> | Queries both backends, renders dashboards | <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> | Deployment |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Key asymmetry to remember:**</span> Promtail <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pushes**</span> logs to Loki; Prometheus <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pulls**</span> metrics\nfrom targets. Two different transport models, one shared label model.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 3 — Logs: Loki + Promtail</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 3.1 Why Loki?</span>\n\nLoki is a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**horizontally-scalable, multi-tenant log aggregation system**</span> built by Grafana Labs.\nIt is deliberately different from full-text systems like ELK:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Cost-effective:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**10–20x cheaper**</span> for storage, because Loki <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**indexes only metadata\n  (labels) — not the full text of every log line.**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Storage:**</span> Logs are compressed into <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**chunks**</span> and stored in <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cheap object storage**</span>\n  (or a local filesystem in labs).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Querying:**</span> You query with <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**LogQL**</span>, a language <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*deliberately designed to be similar to PromQL*</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Integration:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Native Grafana integration**</span> (no separate UI needed), and it uses the\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**exact same label model as Kubernetes and Prometheus**</span>.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The mental model:**</span> think of Loki as \"Prometheus for logs.\" Labels identify <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*which stream*</span> a log\nbelongs to (that's what's indexed); the actual text is searched only <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*at query time*</span>. This is the\nroot cause of both its cheapness and its query behavior — label selectors are fast, line filters\nare done on the fly.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 3.2 Loki Architecture — the write path and read path</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```\n WRITE PATH\n container log files ──► Promtail ──► Distributor ──► Ingester ──► Store\n                                        │               │            │\n                                   validates &amp;     batches into   chunks +\n                                   hashes streams  chunks, writes  index\n\n READ PATH\n Grafana ──LogQL──► Querier ──► reads chunks + index from Store/Ingester\n```</span>\n\n| Component | Job |\n|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**1. Promtail**</span> | Ships logs from the nodes |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**2. Distributor**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Validates**</span> and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**hashes**</span> the incoming log streams |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**3. Ingester**</span> | Batches the streams into <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**chunks**</span> and writes them |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**4. Store**</span> | Stores the chunks <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**and the index**</span> (object storage or local disk) |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**5. Querier**</span> | Executes the LogQL queries coming from Grafana |\n\nA <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**log stream**</span> = a unique combination of labels. Every distinct label set is its own stream —\nwhich is why the labels Promtail attaches matter so much.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 3.3 Promtail Workflow — why a DaemonSet, and the 4 steps</span>\n\nPromtail runs as a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**DaemonSet**</span> — <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**one pod per Kubernetes node**</span>. Why? Because container logs\nare <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**files on each node's local filesystem**</span>. A Promtail pod on node A physically cannot read\nnode B's <span class=\"hljs-code\">`/var/log/pods`</span>. Node-local data ⇒ node-local collector.\n\n| Step | Action | Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**1. Discover**</span> | Find pods | Uses <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Kubernetes service discovery**</span> to find every pod running <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**on its node**</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**2. Tail**</span> | Read the logs | Reads container log files directly from <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`/var/log/pods`**</span> — like a <span class=\"hljs-code\">`tail -f`</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**3. Label**</span> | Attach metadata | Attaches Kubernetes metadata as labels: <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**namespace, pod, container, node**</span> — <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*the exact same labels Prometheus uses*</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**4. Ship**</span> | Send to Loki | Batches log lines and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pushes to Loki's distributor over HTTP**</span> |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Why step 3 matters more than it looks:**</span> because Promtail's labels are identical to Prometheus's\nlabels, logs and metrics are cross-correlatable by default. You see a CPU spike on\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">`pod X, namespace Y`</span> in a Prometheus panel → query <span class=\"hljs-code\">`{namespace=\"Y\", pod=\"X\"}`</span> in Loki → read the\nlogs from exactly that window. Zero extra configuration produces this.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 4 — Metrics: Prometheus + Exporters</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 4.1 The pull model</span>\n\nPrometheus is an open-source monitoring system that uses a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pull model**</span>: it <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**scrapes**</span> metrics\nfrom targets on a regular interval, rather than waiting for applications to push data.\n\nPractical consequences of pulling:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Central control:**</span> the scraper decides the cadence and the target list — apps don't need to\n  know where Prometheus lives.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Failure is visible:**</span> a dead target doesn't just leave a gap in the data — its <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span> metric\n  goes to <span class=\"hljs-code\">`0`</span>. You can literally query whether monitoring works.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 4.2 Time series and PromQL</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Storage:**</span> every measurement is a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**time series**</span> = <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**metric name + key/value labels +\n  a sequence of timestamped values.**</span>\n  Example: <span class=\"hljs-code\">`container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace=\"app\", pod=\"api-7d9f\"}`</span> — the labels are\n  the dimensions you aggregate by.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Querying:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**PromQL**</span> — a powerful query language for aggregating, filtering, and computing\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**rates**</span> over time series.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Alerting:**</span> Prometheus pairs with <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Alertmanager**</span>, which turns PromQL conditions into\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**routed alerts**</span> (Slack, email, PagerDuty). <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*(Alertmanager is disabled in this lab's\n  deployment — but the concept stands.)*</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 4.3 Service discovery — no manual IP lists</span>\n\nIn Kubernetes, Prometheus <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**automatically finds targets using service discovery and Custom\nResources (CRDs)**</span>. Nobody maintains a list of pod IPs (pods are ephemeral anyway — the list\nwould be stale in minutes).\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 4.4 The metric sources — know who exposes what</span>\n\n| Component | Where it runs | What it exposes | The question it answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**node-exporter**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**DaemonSet**</span> (one pod per node) | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Host-level**</span> metrics: CPU, memory, disk, network | \"Is the machine itself healthy?\" |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**kube-state-metrics**</span> | Watches the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Kubernetes API**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Object state**</span>: pod status, deployment replicas, restarts | \"Does Kubernetes <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*think*</span> everything is fine?\" |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cAdvisor**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Built into the kubelet**</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Per-container**</span> resource usage (actual CPU/memory each container consumes) | \"Which container is eating resources?\" |\n| <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ServiceMonitor / PodMonitor**</span> | CRDs — not pods at all | Declarative rules for <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**which Services/Pods Prometheus should scrape**</span> | \"How does Prometheus find <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*my app*</span>?\" |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Drill this distinction:**</span> node-exporter = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*machine*</span>, kube-state-metrics = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*Kubernetes objects*</span>,\ncAdvisor = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*containers*</span>. Three layers of \"is it healthy,\" three different components.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ServiceMonitor / PodMonitor:**</span> these CRDs tell Prometheus exactly which Services or Pods to\nscrape. This is what lets <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Helm charts auto-wire**</span> scraping — deploying the CRD <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*is*</span> the\nconfiguration. (Full example in Part 10.)\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 5 — Traces (context for the next step)</span>\n\nA <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**trace**</span> records the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**full journey of a single request**</span> as it moves through multiple services:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```\nAPI Gateway → Auth Service → Order Service → Database Query\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Purpose:**</span> traces show <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**exactly where time was spent**</span> during a request. Metrics tell you\n  <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*that*</span> requests are slow; traces tell you <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*which hop*</span> made them slow.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Tools:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana Tempo, Jaeger, or Zipkin**</span>, with applications instrumented via\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**OpenTelemetry**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Status in this lab:**</span> traces are <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**not deployed here**</span> — they are the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**natural next step**</span>\n  once logs (Loki) and metrics (Prometheus) are in place.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 6 — Visualization: Grafana</span>\n\nGrafana is the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**unified UI for observability**</span>.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Data handling:**</span> Grafana <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**does not store data itself**</span>. It connects to <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Data Sources**</span>\n  (Prometheus, Loki) and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**queries them live**</span> on every panel refresh. Wipe the backends and\n  Grafana has nothing to show — it's a lens, not a database.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**What a Data Source needs:**</span> a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**URL**</span>, <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**authentication**</span>, and the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**query language**</span> it\n  should speak (<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**PromQL**</span> or <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**LogQL**</span>).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Dashboards and panels:**</span> a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Dashboard is a collection of Panels**</span>. Each panel runs a query\n  against one data source and renders the result as a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**graph, table, log stream, or gauge**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Dashboards as code:**</span> dashboards are <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**JSON**</span> — so they can be <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**version-controlled**</span> or\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**imported from `grafana.com/dashboards`**</span> by numeric ID.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Explore mode:**</span> run <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ad-hoc PromQL or LogQL queries**</span> without building a dashboard — the\n  go-to tool for debugging (\"just show me the errors from that namespace <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*right now*</span>\").\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Important deployment detail:**</span> in this stack, Grafana is <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**installed by `kube-prometheus-stack`**</span>,\nnot deployed standalone. That fact is exactly what causes the datasource conflict in Part 8.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 7 — How It All Fits Together in Kubernetes</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The complete data flow:**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Application pods**</span> write logs and (optionally) expose <span class=\"hljs-code\">`/metrics`</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> On every node, <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Promtail**</span> (DaemonSet) tails <span class=\"hljs-code\">`/var/log/pods`</span>, labels each line with\n   Kubernetes metadata, and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pushes**</span> batches to Loki's distributor.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> On every node, <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**node-exporter**</span> (DaemonSet) exposes host metrics; <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cAdvisor**</span> (in the\n   kubelet) exposes per-container metrics; <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**kube-state-metrics**</span> exposes object state.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus pulls**</span> all of those metrics on a scrape interval, discovering targets via\n   Kubernetes service discovery and CRDs (ServiceMonitor/PodMonitor).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana**</span> holds both as data sources and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**queries Prometheus (PromQL)**</span> and\n   <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki (LogQL)**</span> live.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">6.</span> You open Grafana in a browser through a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**port-forward tunnel**</span> and see everything.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Two transports, one label model:**</span> logs are <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*pushed*</span> (Promtail → Loki, HTTP), metrics are\n<span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*pulled*</span> (Prometheus → targets, scrape). But both carry the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**same labels**</span>\n(namespace / pod / container / node), which is what makes the stack feel like one system.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 8 — Deployment: Minikube + Helm</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.1 Helm in one paragraph</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Helm**</span> is a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**package manager for Kubernetes**</span>. It bundles all necessary manifests —\nDeployments, Services, ConfigMaps, CRDs — into <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**\"charts.\"**</span> Installing a chart creates a\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**release**</span>. Charts live in <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**repositories**</span> you add to your host.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.2 Prerequisites and initial setup</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Install Helm**</span> on the host machine.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Add and update the two chart repositories:**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nhelm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts\nhelm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts\nhelm repo update\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Create a dedicated namespace**</span> — best practice, keeps monitoring components separate from\n  application workloads:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nkubectl create namespace observability\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.3 Chart 1: `kube-prometheus-stack` (Prometheus + Grafana + Alertmanager + exporters)</span>\n\nOne chart installs: <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus, Alertmanager, Grafana, node-exporter, kube-state-metrics,\nthe ServiceMonitor/PodMonitor CRDs, and default Kubernetes dashboards.**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**⚠️ Resource management — why the `--set` overrides matter:**</span>\nBy default, this chart <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**requests more CPU and RAM than a small Minikube cluster can handle.**</span>\nIf you install it bare, pods sit in <span class=\"hljs-code\">`Pending`</span> because the scheduler can't fit their resource\n<span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*requests*</span> anywhere. You must <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**drastically trim resource requests and limits for every\ncomponent**</span> with <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set`</span> flags:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nhelm upgrade --install kube-prometheus-stack prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack \\\n  -n observability \\\n  --set alertmanager.enabled=false \\\n  --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.retention=6h \\\n  --set grafana.adminPassword=admin123 \\\n  --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.resources.requests.cpu=100m \\\n  --set prometheus.prometheusSpec.resources.requests.memory=256Mi \\\n  --set grafana.resources.requests.cpu=50m \\\n  --set grafana.resources.requests.memory=128Mi\n  # ...similar trimming --set flags for kube-state-metrics and node-exporter.\n  # (Exact value keys: helm show values prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack)\n```</span>\n\nRationale for each override:\n\n| Override | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Resource requests/limits trimmed on <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**every component**</span> | Chart defaults assume beefy nodes; Minikube pods go <span class=\"hljs-code\">`Pending`</span> otherwise |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`retention=6h`</span> | TSDB storage grows with retention; a lab doesn't need history — 6 hours is plenty for debugging |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`alertmanager.enabled=false`</span> | No alert routing needed in a lab → fewer pods, less RAM |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`adminPassword=admin123`</span> | The lab's Grafana credentials (<span class=\"hljs-code\">`admin`</span> / <span class=\"hljs-code\">`admin123`</span>) |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**🔑 Idempotency rule:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**always use `helm upgrade --install`, never plain `helm install`.**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm upgrade --install`</span> is <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**safe to re-run**</span>: if the release exists it <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**upgrades it in-place**</span>;\nif not, it installs. Plain <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm install`</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**fails**</span> on a second run.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.4 Chart 2: `loki-stack` (Loki + Promtail)</span>\n\nInstalls <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki**</span> for log storage and deploys <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Promtail as a DaemonSet**</span> across the cluster:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nhelm upgrade --install loki-stack grafana/loki-stack \\\n  -n observability \\\n  --set grafana.sidecar.datasources.defaultDatasourceEnabled=false\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.5 ⚠️ The datasource provisioning conflict (the one non-obvious gotcha)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The mechanism, step by step:**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`loki-stack`</span> ships <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**its own Grafana data-source ConfigMap**</span> for Loki.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> The <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana sidecar**</span> (running in the Grafana installed by <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span>)\n   <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**auto-discovers**</span> datasource ConfigMaps and loads them. Nice — Loki appears in Grafana\n   with zero manual configuration.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The conflict:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span> marks <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus**</span> as the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**default**</span> datasource.\n   <span class=\"hljs-code\">`loki-stack`</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**also tries to mark Loki as default**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> Grafana allows <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**exactly one default datasource**</span>. Two defaults = <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**\"Datasource provisioning\n   error\"**</span> → Grafana <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**crashes → restarts → crashes → `CrashLoopBackOff`**</span>.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The fix:**</span> when installing Loki, set\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`grafana.sidecar.datasources.defaultDatasourceEnabled=false`**</span> — Loki still becomes a\ndatasource, just <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**not the default**</span> (Prometheus stays default).\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Emergency repair if it's already broken:**</span> find the extra datasource ConfigMap with\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get configmap -n observability`</span> and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**remove the `isDefault: true`**</span> flag\n(then restart the Grafana pod).\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.6 Accessing Grafana through port-forward</span>\n\nHelm installs Grafana as a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`ClusterIP` Service**</span> — reachable only from <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*inside*</span> the cluster,\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**not from your Windows/WSL host**</span>. You must tunnel:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nkubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 -n observability \\\n  svc/kube-prometheus-stack-grafana 3000:80\n```</span>\n\nThen open <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`http://localhost:3000`**</span> → login <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`admin` / `admin123`**</span>.\n\nDecoding the command:\n\n| Piece | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`svc/kube-prometheus-stack-grafana`</span> | The Service to tunnel to |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`-n observability`</span> | The namespace the Service lives in |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`3000:80`</span> | <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**local port 3000 → Service port 80**</span> |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--address 0.0.0.0`</span> | Bind the forward on <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**all host interfaces**</span>, not loopback only — <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**required on Windows/WSL**</span>, otherwise you get <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*connection refused*</span> from the Windows side |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 8.7 Dashboards: what you get vs what you import</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Pre-installed**</span> (shipped by <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span>): cluster compute resources,\n  namespaces, nodes, and the Kubelet — all Prometheus-backed.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Not included:**</span> Loki dashboards. Import community dashboards via the Grafana UI\n  (<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Dashboards → Import → enter ID → Load**</span>):\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">  -</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ID `13639`**</span> — Kubernetes Logs\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">  -</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ID `12611`**</span> — Log volume\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> Or skip dashboards entirely and use <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Explore**</span> (Part 9).\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 9 — Querying the Data: LogQL and PromQL</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Framing:**</span> two backends, two query languages, one UI. LogQL was <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*deliberately designed to be\nsimilar to PromQL*</span>, and both select by the <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*same labels*</span> — learn one, and the other feels familiar.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 9.1 Start in Explore mode</span>\n\nGrafana → <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Explore**</span> → pick a datasource → run ad-hoc queries, no dashboard needed:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> Against <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Loki:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`{namespace=\"observability\"}`</span> → live log streams from every pod in the namespace\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> Against <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`1`</span> = scrape healthy, <span class=\"hljs-code\">`0`</span> = target down\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 9.2 LogQL — structure and examples</span>\n\nEvery LogQL query has two stages: a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**log stream selector**</span> (labels — <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*indexed*</span>, fast) followed by\nan optional <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**filter pipeline**</span> (line filters — evaluated at <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*query time*</span>) and an optional\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**metric wrapper**</span> (turn log counts into numbers).\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```logql\n# 1. Stream selector: all logs from a namespace\n{namespace=\"observability\"}\n\n# Narrow by pod / container (the labels Promtail attached)\n{namespace=\"default\", pod=\"my-app-7d9f8b6c-\", container=\"app\"}\n\n# 2. Line filters: keep / drop lines by content (this is where Loki searches text)\n{namespace=\"observability\"} |= \"error\"\n{namespace=\"observability\"} |= \"error\" != \"timeout\"\n\n# 3. Metric query: count matching log lines per pod over a 5m window\nsum by (pod) (count_over_time({namespace=\"default\"} |= \"error\" [5m]))\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Why this ordering matters:**</span> the label selector runs against the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**index**</span>; the text search runs\nagainst the matching streams <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**at query time**</span>. That is the direct payoff of Loki's\n\"index only labels\" design — and exactly why the cheap storage doesn't mean slow queries <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*if*</span>\nyour labels are chosen well.\n\nNote the shape of stage 3: it converts <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**logs into a metric**</span> (<span class=\"hljs-code\">`count_over_time`</span> over a <span class=\"hljs-code\">`[5m]`</span>\nrange) — the bridge between the two signal types, and structurally a sibling of PromQL's <span class=\"hljs-code\">`rate()`</span>.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 9.3 PromQL — examples (annotated by source component)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```promql\n# Health of every scrape target (1 = up, 0 = down)\nup\n\n# Only the observability stack itself\nup{namespace=\"observability\"}\n\n# Per-container CPU rate over 5m — metric from cAdvisor (via kubelet)\nsum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total[5m]))\n\n# Container memory right now — cAdvisor\nsum by (pod) (container_memory_usage_bytes)\n\n# Node memory available, as a fraction — metric from node-exporter\nnode_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes\n```</span>\n\nTwo PromQL mechanics worth internalizing:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`[5m]`</span> selects a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**range vector**</span> (many points over 5 minutes) — functions like <span class=\"hljs-code\">`rate()`</span>\n  <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*require*</span> a range.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`sum by (pod) (...)`</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**aggregates**</span> across labels, keeping <span class=\"hljs-code\">`pod`</span> as the grouping dimension —\n  the same labels Loki uses, so results line up across both backends.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 9.4 Instant versus trend debugging</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Right now, is it broken?**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span>, or a raw log stream <span class=\"hljs-code\">`{namespace=\"...\"}`</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Trending toward broken?**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`rate()`</span> over <span class=\"hljs-code\">`[5m]`</span>, a count of errors per pod, memory fraction\n  graphed on a pre-built dashboard.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 10 — Application Integration</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 10.1 Logs: zero configuration</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Promtail automatically collects logs from every pod in the cluster**</span> — no annotations, no CRDs,\nnothing. Its DaemonSet + Kubernetes service discovery pick up new pods as they appear. Deploy an\napp, and its logs (already labeled with namespace/pod/container) are in Loki.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 10.2 Metrics: two requirements</span>\n\nTo get Prometheus scraping your application:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The app must expose a `/metrics` endpoint on an `http` port**</span> (client libraries exist for\n   every major language that publish counters/gauges/histograms in Prometheus format).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Create a `ServiceMonitor` Custom Resource**</span> whose label selector matches your application,\n   so Prometheus discovers it through the CRD (Helm/operator auto-wires the rest).\n\nMinimal annotated example:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```yaml\napiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1\nkind: ServiceMonitor\nmetadata:\n  name: my-app\n  namespace: observability\n  labels:\n    release: kube-prometheus-stack   # must match what the Prometheus operator selects\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: my-app                    # selects YOUR app's Service by its labels\n  namespaceSelector:\n    matchNames:\n      - default                      # where the app's Service lives\n  endpoints:\n    - port: http                     # the NAME of the Service port serving /metrics\n      path: /metrics\n      interval: 30s\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`selector.matchLabels`</span> → finds the app's <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Service**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`endpoints.port`</span> → refers to the Service port <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**name**</span> — which is why the requirement says\n  \"on an <span class=\"hljs-code\">`http`</span> port.\"\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`release: kube-prometheus-stack`</span> → the label the operator's discovery rules match on; this is\n  the \"auto-wiring\" Helm charts provide.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Verify it worked:**</span> query <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up{namespace=\"default\"}`</span> in Explore — your app should appear with\nvalue <span class=\"hljs-code\">`1`</span> within a scrape interval or two.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 11 — Troubleshooting Playbook</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*Reason from <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**symptom → diagnosis → root cause → fix**</span>. The diagnostic command sequence matters\nmore than memorizing fixes.*</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### Scenario 1 — Pods stuck in `Pending`</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Symptom:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get pods -n observability`</span> → pods sitting in <span class=\"hljs-code\">`Pending`</span>, not starting.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Diagnose:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n observability`</span> → events show\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`FailedScheduling: Insufficient cpu / memory`**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Root cause:**</span> the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cluster lacks free CPU/memory**</span>. The chart's default resource <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*requests*</span>\n  don't fit on the Minikube node — and the scheduler will not place a pod whose requests don't fit.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Fix:**</span> either <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**lower the resource requests**</span> (the <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set`</span> overrides from Part 8) or\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**give Minikube more capacity**</span> (<span class=\"hljs-code\">`minikube start --cpus=... --memory=...`</span>).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Lesson:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`Pending`</span> = scheduling problem = look at <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*requests vs available*</span>, before anything else.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### Scenario 2 — Grafana `OOMKilled`</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Symptom:**</span> Grafana pod restarts repeatedly; <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl describe pod`</span> shows last state\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`OOMKilled`**</span> (exit code 137).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Root cause:**</span> the container <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**ran out of memory**</span> — it exceeded its own memory <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*limit*</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Diagnose before fixing:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**check the node's free memory first**</span> (<span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl top nodes`</span>) —\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**do not blindly raise the Grafana limit.**</span> If the node itself has no headroom, a bigger limit\n  just moves the out-of-memory kill from the container to the node.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Fix:**</span> if the node has headroom → raise Grafana's memory limit via <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set`</span>; if not → free\n  node capacity (disable components, trim other pods, grow Minikube).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Lesson:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`OOMKilled`</span> = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*limit*</span> problem; node exhaustion = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*capacity*</span> problem. Diagnose which\n  one you have.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### Scenario 3 — Grafana `CrashLoopBackOff` with a provisioning error</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Symptom:**</span> Grafana cycles <span class=\"hljs-code\">`CrashLoopBackOff`</span>; its logs show a\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**\"Datasource provisioning error.\"**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Diagnose:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl logs &lt;grafana-pod&gt; -n observability`</span> for the error text;\n  <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get configmap -n observability`</span> → you'll find <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**two datasource ConfigMaps**</span>\n  (one from each chart) <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**both marked default**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Root cause:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**two datasources marked as default.**</span> Grafana permits exactly <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**one**</span> default;\n  provisioning fails at startup, every time → crash loop. (This is <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*not*</span> a resource problem —\n  a config validation failure can crash-loop a perfectly sized pod.)\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Fix:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**prevention**</span> — install loki-stack with\n  <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set grafana.sidecar.datasources.defaultDatasourceEnabled=false`</span>.\n  <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Repair**</span> — find the extra ConfigMap and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**remove the `isDefault: true`**</span> flag, then restart\n  Grafana (<span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl delete pod`</span> — the Deployment recreates it clean).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Lesson:**</span> read the crash logs — the error message names the subsystem (provisioning), which\n  points straight at ConfigMaps.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### Scenario 4 — \"Connection refused\" from Windows / WSL</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Symptom:**</span> browser on Windows can't reach <span class=\"hljs-code\">`localhost:3000`</span> even though port-forward is running.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Root cause:**</span> the forward was bound to loopback <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**inside the WSL VM only**</span> —\n  the <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--address 0.0.0.0`</span> flag was missing.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Fix:**</span> re-run with the full command:\n  <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 -n observability svc/kube-prometheus-stack-grafana 3000:80`</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">-</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Lesson:**</span> on WSL, your browser and your cluster live on different network boundaries —\n  bind all interfaces so the port relay works.\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### Scenario 5 — \"Grafana shows no data\" (general reasoning flow)</span>\n\nWork the chain in order:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Is the backend pod running?**</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get pods -n observability`</span> (a crashed Loki or\n   Prometheus means no queries can succeed).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Is the datasource alive?**</span> → Grafana → Explore → run <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span> (Prometheus) or\n   <span class=\"hljs-code\">`{namespace=\"observability\"}`</span> (Loki). If <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*Explore*</span> works but a dashboard doesn't, the problem\n   is the dashboard's query/time-range, not the stack.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Missing app metrics specifically?**</span> → the app has no <span class=\"hljs-code\">`/metrics`</span> endpoint, or the\n   ServiceMonitor selector doesn't match — check <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span> for the target.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Right namespace? Right time range?**</span> → the boring causes are the common ones.\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 12 — Cleanup</span>\n\nRemove the entire stack:\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```bash\nhelm uninstall kube-prometheus-stack -n observability\nhelm uninstall loki-stack            -n observability\nkubectl delete namespace observability\n```</span>\n\nDeleting the namespace removes anything the charts left behind inside it.\n<span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*(Practical note: the CRDs that `kube-prometheus-stack` installed are cluster-scoped and may\nremain after uninstall — delete them explicitly if you want a spotless cluster.)*</span>\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 13 — The Mental Model (Architecture Diagram)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">```\n                        YOUR HOST  (Windows / WSL)\n  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │   Browser ──► http://localhost:3000         (admin/admin123)  │\n  │       ▲                                                       │\n  │       └── kubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 \\            │\n  │             -n observability \\                                │\n  │             svc/kube-prometheus-stack-grafana 3000:80         │\n  └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┘\n                          │  tunnel into the cluster\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ ╪ ━━━━━━━━━━━ MINIKUBE CLUSTER ━━━━━━━━━━━\n                          ▼\n     namespace: observability\n  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │                     GRAFANA  (Deployment)                       │\n  │           the single UI — it stores NO data itself              │\n  │        ▲ queries PromQL                ▲ queries LogQL          │\n  └────────┼───────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┘\n           │                               │\n  ┌────────┴───────────────┐     ┌─────────┴──────────────┐\n  │       PROMETHEUS       │     │         LOKI           │\n  │   pull model · TSDB    │     │  label index + chunk   │\n  │   time series storage  │     │  store (disk/object)   │\n  └────────▲───────────────┘     └─────────▲──────────────┘\n           │ PULL /metrics                 │ PUSH (HTTP, batched)\n           │                        inside Loki:\n           │              Promtail → Distributor → Ingester → Store\n           │                                 (Querier serves Grafana)\n  ┌────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n  │                EVERY NODE   (DaemonSets)                   │\n  │  ┌────────────────┐        ┌────────────────────────┐      │\n  │  │ node-exporter  │        │        Promtail        │      │\n  │  │ host metrics:  │        │ tails /var/log/pods,   │      │\n  │  │ cpu/mem/disk/  │        │ attaches labels        │      │\n  │  │ net            │        │ (ns/pod/ctr/node),     │      │\n  │  └────────────────┘        │ ships to Distributor   │      │\n  │                            └────────────────────────┘      │\n  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n  ALSO FEEDING PROMETHEUS:\n   • kube-state-metrics ── watches K8s API ──► pod status / replicas / restarts\n   • cAdvisor (inside kubelet) ─────────────► per-container cpu/mem usage\n   • your apps ── /metrics ──(ServiceMonitor CRD)──► auto-discovered &amp; scraped\n```</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**The one-paragraph mental model:**</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; Nodes produce raw signals. <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Promtail pushes logs**</span> into Loki, which indexes <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*only labels*</span> and</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; compresses chunks into storage. <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Prometheus pulls metrics**</span> from node-exporter, cAdvisor,</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; kube-state-metrics, and any app with a `/metrics` endpoint + ServiceMonitor. Both systems speak</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**same label language**</span> (namespace/pod/container/node). <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Grafana stores nothing**</span> — it</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; queries both backends live (PromQL/LogQL) and renders them for humans. You reach Grafana from</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-quote\">&gt; your laptop through a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**port-forward**</span> because its ClusterIP isn't externally reachable.</span>\n\n---\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">## Part 14 — Interview Prep</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 14.1 Concept questions (with distilled answers)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**What is observability?**</span>\n   The ability to understand what's happening inside a system by examining the data it produces —\n   <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**without shipping new code to ask a new question.**</span> Pillars: logs, metrics, traces, visualization.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Logs vs metrics vs traces — when do you use each?**</span>\n   Logs = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*what happened and what did it say*</span> (exact events). Metrics = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*how much/how fast/how\n   often*</span> (trends, rates, alerting). Traces = <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*where did the time go*</span> (latency per hop of one\n   request). Debug flow: metric alerts → trace locates the slow hop → log shows the exception.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Why is Loki 10–20x cheaper than full-text log systems?**</span>\n   It indexes <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**only metadata (labels), not the full text**</span> of log lines; text is searched at\n   query time. Chunks are compressed into <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cheap object storage**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Pull vs push — explain Prometheus's model vs Promtail's.**</span>\n   Prometheus <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pulls**</span> (scrapes targets on an interval — central control of cadence, and a dead\n   target is visible via <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up=0`</span>). Promtail <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**pushes**</span> logs (HTTP batches) to Loki's distributor.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Why are Promtail and node-exporter DaemonSets?**</span>\n   Their data is <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**node-local**</span>: container logs live in each node's <span class=\"hljs-code\">`/var/log/pods`</span>; host metrics\n   are per-machine. One pod per node guarantees full coverage.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">6.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**What makes up a Prometheus time series?**</span>\n   A <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**metric name + key/value labels + a sequence of timestamped values.**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">7.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**node-exporter vs kube-state-metrics vs cAdvisor?**</span>\n   Host-level machine metrics (DaemonSet) vs Kubernetes <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*object state*</span> from the API server\n   (pod status, replicas, restarts) vs per-container resource usage built into the kubelet.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">8.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**What are ServiceMonitor/PodMonitor and why do they exist?**</span>\n   CRDs that <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**declaratively**</span> tell Prometheus which Services/Pods to scrape — no manual IP\n   lists; deploying the CRD <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*is*</span> the configuration (Helm charts auto-wire it).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">9.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Tell me about a bug you debugged in this stack.**</span> <span class=\"hljs-emphasis\">*(the star story)*</span>\n   Grafana <span class=\"hljs-code\">`CrashLoopBackOff`</span> with a datasource provisioning error: both <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kube-prometheus-stack`</span>\n   and <span class=\"hljs-code\">`loki-stack`</span> provisioned a datasource marked <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**default**</span>; Grafana allows exactly one.\n   Diagnosed via pod logs + <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get configmap`</span>; fixed by\n   <span class=\"hljs-code\">`grafana.sidecar.datasources.defaultDatasourceEnabled=false`</span> (or removing <span class=\"hljs-code\">`isDefault: true`</span>\n   from the extra ConfigMap).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">10.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Why `helm upgrade --install` instead of `helm install`?**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">    Idempotency — re-running **upgrades an existing release in place** instead of failing.\n11. **Why port-forward instead of a Service type like NodePort/LoadBalancer?**\n    Grafana is installed as **ClusterIP** (cluster-internal only). Port-forward is the\n    zero-configuration dev/lab access path; NodePort/LoadBalancer/Ingress are what you'd use to\n    expose it properly. On Windows/WSL you **must** pass `--address 0.0.0.0`.\n12. **Why drop Prometheus retention to 6h in a lab?**\n    TSDB storage grows with retention; a lab needs no history, so 6h saves significant disk.\n13. **How do logs and metrics link together in this stack?**\n    **Same label model** — Promtail attaches the exact labels Prometheus uses\n    (namespace/pod/container/node), so a metric anomaly on a pod maps directly to that pod's log stream.\n14. **What's the natural next step for this stack?**\n    **Traces** — deploy Grafana Tempo (or Jaeger/Zipkin) and instrument apps with OpenTelemetry,\n    completing all three signals.\n</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 14.2 Command cheat sheet</span>\n\n| Command | What it does / tells you |\n|---|---|\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts`</span> | Add chart repo |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm repo add grafana https://grafana.github.io/helm-charts`</span> | Add chart repo |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm repo update`</span> | Refresh repo indexes |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl create namespace observability`</span> | Dedicated namespace for the stack |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm upgrade --install &lt;release&gt; &lt;chart&gt; -n observability --set ...`</span> | Idempotent install/upgrade |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm list -n observability`</span> | Installed releases |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm uninstall &lt;release&gt; -n observability`</span> | Remove a release |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get pods -n observability`</span> | First health check: Running / Pending / CrashLoopBackOff |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n observability`</span> | Events: FailedScheduling, OOMKilled, restart reason |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n observability`</span> | App-level errors (provisioning error lives here) |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl get configmap -n observability`</span> | Find datasource provisioning ConfigMaps |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl top nodes`</span> / <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl top pods -n observability`</span> | Actual CPU/memory vs requests |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl delete pod &lt;pod&gt; -n observability`</span> | Force a clean restart (e.g., after ConfigMap fix) |\n| <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 -n observability svc/kube-prometheus-stack-grafana 3000:80`</span> | Access Grafana at <span class=\"hljs-code\">`localhost:3000`</span> |\n\n<span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Query one-liners:**</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`{namespace=\"observability\"}`</span> (Loki) · <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up`</span> (Prometheus) ·\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">`sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total[5m]))`</span> ·\n<span class=\"hljs-code\">`sum by (pod) (count_over_time({namespace=\"default\"} |= \"error\" [5m]))`</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-section\">### 14.3 Self-quiz</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> Which Loki component validates and hashes incoming log streams?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> From which path does Promtail read container logs, and as what workload type?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> What three things make up a Prometheus time series?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> Which component exposes per-container CPU/memory, and where does it run?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.</span> Why does Grafana crash-loop when two datasources are default, and what's the exact <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set`</span> fix?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">6.</span> What does <span class=\"hljs-code\">`up == 0`</span> mean?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">7.</span> Why does Grafana itself store no data?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">8.</span> What are the three commands for full cleanup?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">9.</span> Which two community dashboard IDs cover Kubernetes logs and log volume?\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">10.</span> Why must the port-forward use <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--address 0.0.0.0`</span> on Windows/WSL?\n\n<span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;<span class=\"hljs-name\">details</span>&gt;</span></span>\n<span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;<span class=\"hljs-name\">summary</span>&gt;</span></span><span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;<span class=\"hljs-name\">strong</span>&gt;</span></span>Answers<span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;/<span class=\"hljs-name\">strong</span>&gt;</span></span> (click to expand)<span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;/<span class=\"hljs-name\">summary</span>&gt;</span></span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">1.</span> The <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Distributor**</span> (then the Ingester batches streams into chunks, and the Store keeps chunks + index).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">2.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**`/var/log/pods`**</span> on its node; Promtail runs as a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**DaemonSet**</span> (one pod per node, because logs are node-local files).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">3.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**Metric name + key/value labels + a sequence of timestamped values.**</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">4.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**cAdvisor**</span>, built into the <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**kubelet**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">5.</span> Grafana allows exactly <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**one default datasource**</span>; provisioning fails at startup every restart. Fix: <span class=\"hljs-code\">`--set grafana.sidecar.datasources.defaultDatasourceEnabled=false`</span> when installing loki-stack.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">6.</span> A scrape target is <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**down**</span> — Prometheus couldn't scrape it (visible failure is a pull-model benefit).\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">7.</span> It's a <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**visualization layer**</span>: it connects to data sources (Prometheus, Loki) and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**queries them live**</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">8.</span> <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm uninstall kube-prometheus-stack -n observability`</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`helm uninstall loki-stack -n observability`</span> → <span class=\"hljs-code\">`kubectl delete namespace observability`</span>.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">9.</span> <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**13639**</span> (Kubernetes Logs) and <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**12611**</span> (Log volume), imported via the Grafana UI.\n<span class=\"hljs-bullet\">10.</span> Without it, the forward binds to loopback inside WSL only, and the Windows browser gets <span class=\"hljs-strong\">**connection refused**</span>; <span class=\"hljs-code\">`0.0.0.0`</span> binds all host interfaces.\n<span class=\"language-xml\"><span class=\"hljs-tag\">&lt;/<span class=\"hljs-name\">details</span>&gt;</span></span>\n</code></pre>\n<p>Want me to also generate a condensed one-page cheat sheet version, or a set of Anki-style flashcards from the quiz section?</p>\n","toc":[],"words":592,"assets":[]};